    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** The level of formality a speaker chooses.\n\n**2.** The statement a debate argues for and against.\n\n**3.** The proposition.\n\n**4.** An answer that shows why an opponent's argument fails.\n\n**5.** Claim, reason and evidence.\n\n**6.** Any question that cannot be answered yes or no.\n\n**7.** Judges the debate and gives a decision.\n\n**8.** Admitting that a point on the other side is correct.\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** linking\n\n**2.** synthesis\n\n**3.** proof\n\n**4.** information\n\n**5.** straw\n\n**6.** ad hominem\n\n**7.** floor\n\n**8.** Both sides must be arguing about the same thing. If *free education* is left undefined, one side may argue about fees and the other about uniforms, and neither will meet the other.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** Three parts, and the link stated.\n\n**2.** For example: *This matter must be resolved without delay, and I ask for your cooperation.*\n\n**3.** Each must join a previous point to a new one.\n\n**4.** Any versions beginning with what, why or how.\n\n**5.** *Better* must be defined — by results, by cost, by welfare?\n\n**6.** Name the hasty generalisation from a small sample.\n\n**7.** One sentence, in question form, aimed at their evidence.\n\n**8.** (a) ad hominem (b) straw man\n\n**9.** The concession must be genuine, and the answer must still stand.\n\n**10.** Keep order, give the floor fairly, keep to the point and to time, summarise.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Marked on the evidence and the links.\n\n**2.** Arguing against yourself is the exercise.\n\n**3.** The proposition must show the motion is true; leaving it undecided is enough for the opposition.\n\n**4.** Six statements, the difference visible in each pair.\n\n**5.** Marked on the separation of the decision from the adjudicator's own opinion.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n\n\n**1.** Claim, reason, evidence; the link states how the evidence supports the claim. **[3]**\n\n**2.** Three distinct parts. **[3]**\n\n**3.** The statement debated; without definitions the two sides argue about different things. **[3]**\n\n**4.** They said; but; because; therefore. **[4]**\n\n**5.** (a) ad hominem (b) straw man **[4]**\n\n**6.** The duty to prove the motion; it lies with the proposition. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: terms defined (2), three arguments with all three parts (3), links stated (2), a four-step rebuttal (2), an honest concession (1). A speech that asserts three times without evidence earns the definition marks only.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **Points of information** are where debates are won and lost. Insist that every speaker takes at least one; a speaker who refuses them all should be marked down.\n\n- **The discussion.** Synthesis is the hardest of the three requirements; model it once before the groups begin.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Contradiction is not rebuttal.** This is the single most useful thing to teach in the chapter, and it transfers directly to the essay paper.\n\n- **Straw men** are usually accidental at this age; treat them as a fault of listening rather than of honesty.\n\n- **Register.** Learners over-correct into stiffness. Formal does not mean long.\n\n- **Challenge 5** needs a debate to have been watched — the class debate in the speaking section will serve.\n\n\n\n*Strand 1: Oral Language (Listening and Speaking)*"
