    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** A factual film about real people, places or events.\n\n**2.** A voice heard while something else is shown on screen.\n\n**3.** Words on screen naming or explaining what is shown.\n\n**4.** A short recorded extract of someone speaking.\n\n**5.** Figures and who said them.\n\n**6.** A one-sided treatment of a subject.\n\n**7.** The person being questioned.\n\n**8.** For example: *How do you know that?*\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** audio-visual\n\n**2.** footage\n\n**3.** commentary\n\n**4.** attribution\n\n**5.** initiating\n\n**6.** building\n\n**7.** probing\n\n**8.** A camera can only point one way. Choosing which people, places and moments appear tells the viewer what matters, before a word of commentary is spoken.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** Footage, commentary, voice-over, captions, sound bites, interviews, music, graphics — any six.\n\n**2.** Marked on whether the fourth column has anything in it.\n\n**3.** Three figures, three attributions.\n\n**4.** Two genuine differences between image and word.\n\n**5.** Music, choice of images, emotive words, an expert's authority, repetition, ordering.\n\n**6.** Two genuinely absent groups.\n\n**7.** It must press on evidence or on selection.\n\n**8.** Three statements correctly marked.\n\n**9.** Central idea, main evidence, count given.\n\n**10.** Figures, sources and questions; what remains is an impression.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Differences of selection, order, emphasis or language.\n\n**2.** Through selection, order, emphasis and omission, none of which requires a false statement.\n\n**3.** The script must show what is on screen as well as what is said.\n\n**4.** The exercise proves the point of Challenge 2.\n\n**5.** Marked on the record.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** Any six with contributions. **[4]**\n\n\n\n**2.** Facts and figures; who said it; what the pictures showed; my questions. **[4]**\n\n**3.** Naming the source; without it the figure cannot be checked or weighed. **[3]**\n\n**4.** Music, image selection, emotive language, expert authority, repetition, ordering. **[3]**\n\n**5.** Through selection, emphasis and omission. **[3]**\n\n**6.** Starting a line; adding to another's point; pressing for evidence or explanation. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: four facts with sources (3), image separated from word (2), two techniques with examples (2), an absent voice named (2), a genuine unanswered question (1). A review that reports only what was said, with no notice of what was shown, loses a third of the marks.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **The ninety-second report.** If the group cannot state the central claim, the report gave impressions rather than content.\n\n- **The discussion tally.** Building on is the requirement learners find hardest; most groups produce parallel speeches rather than a discussion.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Radio works as well as television** for every task here except the image column; where screens are unavailable, substitute a recorded interview and drop that column.\n\n- **Selection, not falsehood.** This is the central idea of the chapter and it will be tested again in Chapter 4.\n\n- **Four columns.** Learners resist the fourth column until they try to write the review without it.\n\n- **Core 2 to 9** all depend on one broadcast; choose it carefully and use it for the whole chapter.\n\n\n\n*Strand 1: Oral Language (Listening and Speaking)*"
