    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** Three.\n\n**2.** On the first syllable.\n\n**3.** /str/\n\n**4.** /skt/\n\n**5.** Voiced.\n\n**6.** light / right — or any correct pair.\n\n**7.** A word of one syllable.\n\n**8.** The second.\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** lateral\n\n**2.** approximant\n\n**3.** cluster\n\n**4.** insertion\n\n**5.** reduction\n\n**6.** syllable\n\n**7.** shift\n\n**8.** The stressed word is the one being contrasted with something else. *I* didn't take it points at someone else; I didn't take *it* points at something else.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** For example: light, follow, tall.\n\n**2.** red, arrive, sorry; in non-rhotic accents final *r* is not pronounced before a pause.\n\n**3.** right, correct, grass, yet\n\n**4.** /str/, /spl/, /skr/, /lfθs/, /rldz/\n\n**5.** The first two insert a vowel into the cluster; the third reduces and reorders it.\n\n**6.** 3, 3, 5, 3, 5\n\n**7.** As shown.\n\n**8.** First syllable for the nouns, second for the verbs.\n\n**9.** (a) someone else did (b) something else was taken (c) it was not taken but perhaps moved or borrowed\n\n**10.** Marked by ear.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** The reduced consonant carries tense or number, so the grammar of the sentence disappears with the sound.\n\n**2.** Twelve pairs, correctly stressed when read aloud.\n\n**3.** Five meanings from one sentence.\n\n**4.** The suffix decides the stress; e.g. *economy, economic, economist*; *politics, political, politician*.\n\n**5.** Marked on the honesty of the list.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** /str/, /lfθs/, /skt/ **[3]**\n\n\n\n**2.** Adding a vowel inside a cluster; dropping a consonant from it. **[4]**\n\n**3.** 3, 4, 5; imPORtant, phoTOgrapher, opporTUnity **[3]**\n\n**4.** First syllable for nouns, second for verbs. **[3]**\n\n**5.** Someone else did; something else was taken. **[4]**\n\n**6.** The /t/ carries the past tense. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: ten clusters used and underlined (3), four final clusters carrying grammar (3), four stress-shift words used in both classes (3), stress marked (1). Mark the underlining by sound rather than by spelling: *box* ends in a cluster although it is written with one letter.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **Mark the three things only.** Do not deduct for a Ghanaian accent; deduct where a cluster has lost a consonant that carries meaning.\n\n- **The dictation.** Back to back, so lips cannot be read.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Clusters carry grammar.** By Basic 9 this should be automatic; it is worth two or three minutes at the start of every lesson.\n\n- **Stress shift** appears in the BECE and is easily taught; twelve pairs learnt properly cover most of what is asked.\n\n- **Sentence stress** is the most enjoyable part of the chapter and the least practised. Core 9 should be done aloud before it is written.\n\n- **Challenge 5** needs a recording device; a shared telephone will do.\n\n\n\n### Review 1 — Oral Language (Listening and Speaking)\n\n*Chapters 1 to 3. Time: 40 minutes. Total: 30 marks.*\n\n**1.** Claim, reason, evidence; the link states how the evidence supports the claim. **[3]**\n\n**2.** They said; but; because; therefore. Contradiction is itself a claim and needs the same three parts. **[4]**\n\n**3.** ad hominem; straw man **[3]**\n\n**4.** The duty to prove the motion; it lies with the proposition. **[3]**\n\n**5.** Facts and figures; who said it; what the pictures showed; my questions. **[4]**\n\n**6.** Through selection, emphasis and omission. **[3]**\n\n**7.** /str/, /lfθs/, /skt/; the past tense. **[4]**\n\n**8.** 3, 4, 5; imPORtant, phoTOgrapher, opporTUnity **[3]**\n\n**9.** First syllable for nouns, second for verbs. **[3]**\n\n\n\n*Strand 2: Reading*"
