    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** To tell readers what happened.\n\n**2.** An article giving the newspaper's own opinion.\n\n**3.** A statement issued by an organisation to be reported.\n\n**4.** The position from which a writer sees a subject.\n\n**5.** Who wrote it, when, how they know, who else says so — any one.\n\n**6.** Confirmation from a second, independent source.\n\n**7.** A statement made about a whole group.\n\n**8.** Something a source stands to gain.\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** feature\n\n**2.** column\n\n**3.** hasty\n\n**4.** false\n\n**5.** dilemma\n\n**6.** popularity\n\n**7.** circular\n\n**8.** A press release is written by the organisation it is about, to be believed. A news report should have been checked by someone with nothing to gain from it.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** News report, feature, editorial, opinion column, press release.\n\n**2.** (a) news report or press release (b) feature (c) editorial\n\n**3.** Three of each, correctly sorted.\n\n**4.** Hasty generalisation, false cause, false dilemma, appeal to popularity, circular reasoning.\n\n**5.** (a) hasty generalisation (b) false cause (c) false dilemma\n\n**6.** (a) appeal to popularity (b) circular reasoning\n\n**7.** Four answers, drawn from the text.\n\n**8.** A second, independent source must be named.\n\n**9.** Something the source stands to gain; two plausible examples.\n\n**10.** Marked on completeness.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Differences of fact, source, emphasis, order or language.\n\n**2.** Marked on the identification.\n\n**3.** The fallacies must be named.\n\n**4.** An unnamed many cannot be checked or weighed; a named source can be examined for interest and for knowledge.\n\n**5.** An editorial with no judgements is a report; with no facts it is an assertion.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** News report, feature, editorial, opinion column, press release. **[4]**\n\n\n\n**2.** Issued by the body it concerns, to be believed; it serves that body's interest. **[3]**\n\n**3.** Hasty generalisation, false cause, false dilemma, appeal to popularity, circular reasoning. **[3]**\n\n**4.** hasty generalisation; false cause; false dilemma **[3]**\n\n**5.** Who wrote it and what do they gain; when; how do they know; who else says so. **[4]**\n\n**6.** Confirmation by an independent source; six repetitions of one source remain one source. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: both sources correctly classified (2), facts separated from judgements (2), four credibility questions applied to both (3), a fallacy correctly named (2), a reasoned choice (1). A candidate who prefers the source they agree with, without reasons drawn from the four questions, loses the last three marks.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **The challenging question** should press on why the chosen source is more credible, not on the subject itself.\n\n- **The fallacy hunt** works best with real arguments cut from newspapers or heard on radio.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Five fallacies, learnt by name.** They recur in the BECE comprehension and in every debate; naming them is half the work of answering them.\n\n- **Press releases** reported verbatim are common; Challenge 2 is easy to set from any week's papers.\n\n- **Credibility is not agreement.** Learners must practise finding a source credible while disagreeing with it.\n\n- **Core 10 and Challenge 1** run over two weeks; set them at the start of the term.\n\n\n\n*Strand 2: Reading*"
