    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** Searching a text for one particular piece of information.\n\n**2.** Reading quickly for the general sense.\n\n**3.** Cause and effect, problem and solution, compare and contrast, chronological, general to particular — any one.\n\n**4.** A comparison using *like* or *as*.\n\n**5.** Language appealing to the five senses.\n\n**6.** The associations a word carries beyond its plain meaning.\n\n**7.** Something that stands for an idea beyond itself.\n\n**8.** Because, therefore, consequently, as a result — any one.\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** structure\n\n**2.** problem\n\n**3.** general\n\n**4.** style\n\n**5.** purpose\n\n**6.** domain-specific\n\n**7.** nuance\n\n**8.** Naming a device and describing a structure can both be done without understanding the text. Effect asks what the choice does to the reader, which cannot be answered from a list.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** (a) scanning (b) skimming (c) close reading\n\n**2.** Cause and effect (because); problem and solution (however, the answer is); compare and contrast (whereas); chronological (then, next); general to particular (for instance).\n\n**3.** Three structures with evidence.\n\n**4.** A problem stated in paragraph one predicts a solution; a comparison predicts the second term.\n\n**5.** Two three-move answers.\n\n**6.** Two explanations, each with the words quoted.\n\n**7.** The symbol and the idea must both be named.\n\n**8.** Dying or dead; the clue is the explanation that follows.\n\n**9.** Weather; land or employment; water or schools; money sent home by migrants.\n\n**10.** Each pair shares a meaning and differs in approval.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Function, not content: *concedes*, *gives evidence*, *narrows*.\n\n**2.** Weak names the device; fair describes it; strong says what it does to the reader, with words quoted.\n\n**3.** The loss must be named precisely.\n\n**4.** Twelve correct uses.\n\n**5.** Four genuine shifts of connotation.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n\n\n**1.** Cause and effect; problem and solution; compare and contrast; chronological; general to particular. **[4]**\n\n**2.** Scanning; skimming; close reading. **[3]**\n\n**3.** What the writer does; how it works; what it does to the reader. **[3]**\n\n**4.** A three-move answer, with the effect named. **[4]**\n\n**5.** Dying or dead; the explanation that follows. **[3]**\n\n**6.** Same denotation, opposite approval in each pair. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: purpose and audience (1), structure traced with quoted signals (3), three language choices in all three moves (3), a symbol identified (1), four connotations explained (2). An analysis that names devices without saying what they do earns half the language marks at most.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **The three-minute analysis.** Require the words to be quoted aloud; a learner who cannot find them has claimed an effect that is not there.\n\n- **Naming paragraph functions** in one word is a fast drill and prepares Chapter 6 directly.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Effect is the BECE discriminator.** Most candidates name; few explain. Three moves, every time.\n\n- **Structure predicts.** A learner who recognises problem and solution in paragraph one has already half-read the article.\n\n- **Domain-specific vocabulary** is where reading across the curriculum pays; take examples from Science and Social Studies.\n\n- **Core 3 to 9** work best on one shared article.\n\n\n\n*Strand 2: Reading*"
