    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** port\n\n**2.** There\n\n**3.** stopped\n\n**4.** visited\n\n**5.** That the word is not suitable for formal writing.\n\n**6.** rain\n\n**7.** principle\n\n**8.** A word's plain dictionary meaning.\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** context\n\n**2.** connotation\n\n**3.** nuance\n\n**4.** collocation\n\n**5.** register\n\n**6.** homophones\n\n**7.** label\n\n**8.** Nothing in the grammar forbids it and every listener knows what is meant. It is wrong only because English speakers say *heavy rain*, and collocations must be learnt rather than reasoned out.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** Definition, example, contrast, cause and effect, restatement.\n\n**2.** (a) dying or dead — cause and effect (b) talkative — contrast\n\n**3.** predict, dictate, contradict, dictionary; reject, inject, project, eject; convert, revert, divert, invert\n\n**4.** un-mistake-able; dis-agree-ment; inter-nation-al-ise.\n\n**5.** Each pair shares a meaning and differs in approval.\n\n**6.** heavy rain; make a decision; do homework; fast food\n\n**7.** children, obtain or receive, a great deal of, resolve\n\n**8.** Marked in four sentences of the learner's own.\n\n**9.** stopping, visiting, beginning, travelling, preferring, opening\n\n**10.** knee, write, doubt, island, honest, castle — any six.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Marked on the record of errors, not on the number right.\n\n**2.** Three genuine examples.\n\n**3.** The grammar should be identical.\n\n**4.** Four distinct senses.\n\n**5.** Double a final consonant after a single stressed vowel: *begin/beginning*, but *open/opening*.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** Definition, example, contrast, cause and effect, restatement. **[3]**\n\n\n\n**2.** Dying or dead; cause and effect. **[3]**\n\n**3.** predict, dictate, contradict, dictionary; un-mistake-able. **[4]**\n\n**4.** Same denotation, opposite approval in each pair. **[3]**\n\n**5.** heavy rain; make a decision. Collocations are learnt by use, not derived. **[3]**\n\n**6.** stopping, beginning, travelling, opening; double after a single stressed vowel. **[4]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: twelve words used correctly (3), four built from roots (2), four synonym choices justified (2), six correct collocations (2), formal register maintained throughout (1). A word used in the wrong sense costs more than a word not used at all.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **The talk.** Mark sense and register, not fluency. A learner who uses *moribund* correctly once has done more than one who uses six long words loosely.\n\n- **The register ladder** shows learners that formality is a scale rather than a switch.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Collocation is the hardest thing** for a second-language writer and the least teachable by rule. Read aloud, and keep a collocation wall.\n\n- **Usage labels** are ignored by most learners; Guided Practice 2 is worth a full lesson with real dictionaries in hand.\n\n- **The doubling rule** must include the stress condition, or *opening* becomes *openning*.\n\n- **Challenge 1** runs alongside the reading record from Chapter 4.\n\n\n\n### Review 3 — Grammar Usage\n\n*Chapters 7 to 10. Time: 55 minutes. Total: 30 marks.*\n\n**1.** A clause has a subject and verb of its own; the head word is *man*. **[3]**\n\n**2.** much advice; fewer pupils; a little money **[3]**\n\n**3.** postpone; visit or invite; stop trying; care for. *Call on* and *look after* are inseparable. **[4]**\n\n**4.** will be; had; would have come **[3]**\n\n**5.** Without commas only the payers were admitted; with commas all had paid. **[3]**\n\n**6.** has; is **[2]**\n\n**7.** The market was closed by the Assembly; when the doer is unknown, unimportant or obvious. **[4]**\n\n**8.** well-known, five-year-old, twenty-three, two-thirds; a man-eating fish **[4]**\n\n**9.** Approval against disapproval; stopping, beginning, travelling, opening. **[4]**\n\n\n\n*Strand 4: Writing*"
