    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** A group of words with a subject and a verb.\n\n**2.** One that could stand alone as a sentence.\n\n**3.** and, but, or, so, for, nor, yet — any one.\n\n**4.** because, although, when, if, since, while — any one.\n\n**5.** The sentence carrying the paragraph's central idea.\n\n**6.** One.\n\n**7.** However — or but, whereas.\n\n**8.** The way the parts of a text hold together.\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** dependent\n\n**2.** compound\n\n**3.** complex\n\n**4.** compound-complex\n\n**5.** major\n\n**6.** unity\n\n**7.** coherence\n\n**8.** Coherence is the order of the ideas: whether one leads to the next. Cohesion is the wording that shows the links. A paragraph can be full of *however* and *therefore* and still be incoherent.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** simple; compound; complex; compound-complex\n\n**2.** Four correct types.\n\n**3.** For example: *Because the bridge had gone and the road was closed, the traders walked.*\n\n**4.** Marked on rhythm and on the relations made explicit.\n\n**5.** Topic, major supports, minor supports, closing.\n\n**6.** Unity, coherence, cohesion, completeness.\n\n**7.** The sentence must be the one leaving the single idea.\n\n**8.** Attach both the pronoun and the demonstrative.\n\n**9.** Contrast; result; comparison; concession; summary.\n\n**10.** Deductive states the idea first; inductive builds to it. Deductive suits argument; inductive suits persuasion of a hostile reader.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Three versions and three effects.\n\n**2.** Marked on the diagnosis, not the repair.\n\n**3.** The signal must be a word or a sentence, not a gap.\n\n**4.** The devices claim relations that the ideas do not have; the order remains wrong.\n\n**5.** Marked on the honesty of the marking.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n\n\n**1.** Simple, compound, complex, compound-complex. **[4]**\n\n**2.** The first can stand alone; the second cannot. **[2]**\n\n**3.** Unity, coherence, cohesion, completeness. **[4]**\n\n**4.** A major carries a main reason or piece of evidence; a minor develops the sentence before it. **[3]**\n\n**5.** Both the pronoun and the demonstrative must be attached. **[4]**\n\n**6.** Idea first against idea last; the inductive suits a hostile reader. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: topic sentences underlined and genuine (2), MAJ and MIN correctly marked (2), all four sentence types (2), four transitional devices at most (1), four tests passed in every paragraph (3). The cap on transitional devices is deliberate: learners at this level over-use them and mistake them for structure.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **The one-sentence test** is the fastest diagnosis of unity available, and it costs a minute.\n\n- **Counting sentence types aloud** shows learners that they speak in two types and write in one.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Coherence before cohesion.** Learners add *however* to paragraphs whose order is wrong. Fix the order first.\n\n- **The four tests** should become a marking shorthand: U, CH, CO, CM in the margin.\n\n- **Sentence variety** is best taught by rewriting, not by definition — Challenge 1 does more than any lesson on clause types.\n\n- **Core 7** needs a paragraph supplied by the teacher, ideally one with a stray sentence.\n\n\n\n*Strand 4: Writing*"
