    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** A scene from an earlier time inserted into a narrative.\n\n**2.** A hint of what is to come later.\n\n**3.** The strongest point on the other side.\n\n**4.** may, might, could, should, must — any one.\n\n**5.** Stating a claim with the right degree of caution.\n\n**6.** The feeling a passage creates in the reader.\n\n**7.** Writing without stopping, to find out what you think.\n\n**8.** The writer's own opinion.\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** selective\n\n**2.** frontloading\n\n**3.** climactic\n\n**4.** flashback\n\n**5.** mental\n\n**6.** rejoinder\n\n**7.** objectivity\n\n**8.** It claims exactly as much as the evidence supports, so a reader cannot overturn it with one exception. *Everyone knows* invites a reader to think of somebody who does not.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** Three details at most, each carrying fact and feeling.\n\n**2.** The named feeling must be absent.\n\n**3.** The return must be signalled.\n\n**4.** A hint, not an announcement.\n\n**5.** Five distinct parts.\n\n**6.** The counter-argument must be one an opponent would recognise.\n\n**7.** Use modal and mental verbs so that one exception cannot overturn the claim.\n\n**8.** The facts must survive; the loaded words must go.\n\n**9.** The strongest material first, and the weight at the end.\n\n**10.** Writing without stopping for a set time, to break a blank page and find the argument.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** Three genuinely different treatments.\n\n**2.** The feelings must survive without being named.\n\n**3.** Answering a straw man teaches the reader that the writer has not understood the opposition.\n\n**4.** Three genuine examples.\n\n**5.** All three present and marked.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** Few details carrying most weight, plus a rewrite of three details at most. **[4]**\n\n\n\n**2.** Three definitions and one example. **[3]**\n\n**3.** Four distinct parts. **[3]**\n\n**4.** The strongest point against; a straw man shows the writer has not understood the opposition. **[4]**\n\n**5.** A modal or mental verb, matched to the evidence. **[3]**\n\n**6.** The facts kept, the loaded words removed. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: plan attached (1), selective detail with mood built rather than named (3), a marked flashback or foreshadowing (1), position with three reasons and evidence (2), the strongest counter-argument answered (2), hedging matched to evidence (1). Both pieces must be on one subject.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **Naming the detail.** If the class can name the mood but not the detail that made it, the writer stated the feeling somewhere; find the sentence.\n\n- **Restating the opponent** before answering is the rule to keep all year. It ends straw men faster than any explanation.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Hedging is not weakness.** Learners think confidence means absolutes. Show them how one counter-example destroys *always*.\n\n- **Mood built from detail** is the hardest skill in Basic 9 composition and the most rewarded in the BECE narrative question.\n\n- **Free writing** should be timed — five minutes, no stopping, no correcting — and never marked.\n\n- **The double writing task** needs two sittings.\n\n\n\n*Strand 4: Writing*"
