    "body": "#### Recall\n\n**1.** Top right.\n\n**2.** Yours faithfully.\n\n**3.** A copy has been sent to the person named.\n\n**4.** The written record of what a meeting did.\n\n**5.** The item at the end of an agenda for matters not listed.\n\n**6.** What, when, where, who to contact — any two.\n\n**7.** A decision recording who will do what, and by when.\n\n**8.** Tripling, rhetorical question, parallel structure, inclusive language — any one.\n\n#### Key words\n\n**1.** section\n\n**2.** line\n\n**3.** agenda\n\n**4.** arising\n\n**5.** resolution\n\n**6.** tripling\n\n**7.** inclusive\n\n**8.** Minutes are read by people deciding what to do next. A record of who said what is unusable; a record of what was decided, by whom and by when, can be acted on.\n\n#### Core practice\n\n**1.** The seven parts, with headings inside the body.\n\n**2.** A heading and a request stated first.\n\n**3.** It names who else received the letter; used when another office must know.\n\n**4.** Opening, apologies, matters arising, three items, any other business, date of next meeting.\n\n**5.** Decision recorded, not discussion.\n\n**6.** A resolution records what was decided; an action point records who must do it and by when.\n\n**7.** Short, specific and signed.\n\n**8.** What, when, where, who it is for, cost, contact.\n\n**9.** An opening that earns attention and a closing that lands.\n\n**10.** Tripling, rhetorical question, parallel structure, inclusive language, emotive adjective.\n\n#### Challenge\n\n**1.** All parts present, business stated first.\n\n**2.** Decisions and action points, not discussion.\n\n**3.** All four devices present and marked.\n\n**4.** Persuasion gives the listener the evidence and lets them decide; manipulation hides it or works only on feeling.\n\n**5.** Marked on the reasons.\n\n#### Exam-style\n\n**1.** The seven parts; section headings let a busy reader find the request. **[4]**\n\n\n\n**2.** The business of the letter; who else has received it. **[3]**\n\n**3.** Heading; present and apologies; matters arising; items; any other business; date of next meeting. **[3]**\n\n**4.** What was decided against who must do it and by when. **[3]**\n\n**5.** Tripling, rhetorical question, parallel structure, inclusive language, emotive adjective. **[4]**\n\n**6.** What, when, where, who it is for, cost, contact. **[3]**\n\n#### Writing task — marking guidance\n\nTen marks: letter layout with four section headings (3), subject line, copy line and contact (2), minutes recording decisions in all six parts (2), three action points each with a name and a date (2), a flyer answering all five questions (1). An action point without a name is not an action point.\n\n#### Speaking and listening — what to look for\n\n- **Naming devices as they are heard** trains the ear that will later mark the BECE persuasive question.\n\n- **The real meeting.** Agreeing the minutes at the next session is the part learners remember; it makes the record matter.\n\n- Sign the log only after the task has been performed aloud.\n\n#### Marking notes and common errors\n\n- **Section headings** turn a long letter into one that gets read. They are rarely taught and always useful.\n\n- **Minutes record decisions.** The commonest fault is a transcript of who said what.\n\n- **Persuasion and manipulation.** Challenge 4 is a values lesson as much as a language one; take it seriously.\n\n- **Real documents.** Use the school's own agenda and minutes where the head teacher permits.\n\n\n\n*Strand 4: Writing*"
